#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120625} :activity/id #uuid "faea29b6-21e9-5455-abc1-ad6fe37387f0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121020 :tutorial/content "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations: \n\n  I. Swapping two rows\n\n  II. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\n  III. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix ![](images/fa/fa51d7c585db408c47e8640ae839c1a3bbf94e2b0097e1ade511e5773461f1e8.svg) Taking the determinant, we have\n![](images/bc/bcf5c5dc9f5b7ed3849e719665f802040cf51e3334ae99e25ea7648cbb0f6134.svg)\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5ae8e63-4d2c-5491-9908-96fe94243ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57d74627-70e0-5830-ba95-6cb026f4de4b" :step/math-academy-id 16213 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119589 :question/id #uuid "596aa514-d598-5ffe-affe-f5580d130bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4422"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411967}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"} :step/id #uuid "07d38f85-837d-577c-a3fa-ed4adb824e28" :step/math-academy-id 16214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120704}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119590 :question/id #uuid "89152146-8f04-5b4f-8436-15b82431c35d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4423"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f11b223-edd2-59b8-b761-164d56e29746" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411971}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Row by a Number"} :step/id #uuid "b2bed0ab-d12e-5d90-bbf5-94e480bceb4c" :step/math-academy-id 16215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119591 :question/id #uuid "6d46c442-4675-5e0e-a834-a8b10ad2b86a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24e5e193-5e30-5480-9e5d-b7b8f1d5de87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440484}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Applying Several Elementary Operations"} :step/id #uuid "9c7f038a-e114-5e98-bc36-49762d4031f6" :step/math-academy-id 16216 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121021 :tutorial/content "Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A),$ the corresponding elementary column operations on determinants have the same properties as the elementary row ones.\n\n- Swapping two columns changes the sign of the determinant.\n\n- Multiplying a column by a number scales the determinant by that number.\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column does not change the determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "bac0b72b-b76a-5a06-9312-d2b7b8ac4247" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3005 :tutorial/title "Column Operations on Determinants"} :step/id #uuid "d5da1bd5-0cc1-56fe-a8d7-36aa6576326a" :step/math-academy-id 16217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121044 :tutorial/content "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=-det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f151b612-1bdb-5723-af50-a48439141fbc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3236 :tutorial/title "Switching Two Rows Changes the Sign of the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "1889d210-a2bc-5893-b7e3-879d8fc8961f" :step/math-academy-id 17635 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121045 :tutorial/content "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n![](images/08/0826c05fe2eabec96065eb2c83228168129b42c309764df4b101f4a520c4a0f9.svg)\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=k\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09c28da6-5340-56f9-bbf5-427a824977ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3237 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Row By a Number Scales the Determinant By That Number"} :step/id #uuid "0cc1f27c-f5d8-5b74-9602-32ce4d87b799" :step/math-academy-id 17658 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119665 :question/id #uuid "cbd9b4bc-146a-57f7-a03f-f045f86f3c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-6143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a0ab53e-79ff-5520-bb50-069fd381d29d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483371}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Row Operation Given the Connection Between Two Determinants"} :step/id #uuid "e1ec39b0-859a-5c8a-a8ca-f8a0f6371e76" :step/math-academy-id 22905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121061 :tutorial/content "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n![](images/fe/fe02091dde43a8a2405f6b588f619bc9fa3f54b31ac8a1ac39d318761f00cc3e.svg)\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "1c99dfd1-b583-5247-9ac5-c2ada47c0ee9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3880 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix By a Scalar"} :step/id #uuid "ebb7b384-6a1f-56e3-9927-8911a4ec3563" :step/math-academy-id 22922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119666 :question/id #uuid "ce910ef2-c3af-5173-84d4-ee2a4c957954" :question/math-academy-id "e-6148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "272d9a54-ba97-5175-86df-0a78337da7b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440485}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Matrix by a Scalar"} :step/id #uuid "6bb5981c-60d1-56ed-a146-3ef4d9c0f6bb" :step/math-academy-id 22923 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120628}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119994}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409694} :activity/id #uuid "26bcedbd-f502-5256-b322-6d751a0c036d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08098a6b-f06b-51d7-be19-39ddeb566f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409658} {:answer/id #uuid "4da115ec-321e-53f5-9c9a-06b3818508a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409661} {:answer/id #uuid "7105bb69-b089-5e84-ab20-fd40c1edbdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-864" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409667} {:answer/id #uuid "b58e6bc8-29ce-5a41-8721-675b80eb22cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409673} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd30542-3cc5-5b61-a9af-49753027f933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409673} :answer-field/id #uuid "04759499-638f-5548-ad9a-51762b698235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fded0a2c-cd1a-5de1-a671-20ea991daecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-72737" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(A)=-4.$ What is the value of $det(6A)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=6$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(6A) & ={6}^{2}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-144.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0f93ad19-15c5-55b8-98ea-d5bcee8f0a3a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "403dcee3-3eac-5cf3-a329-0558750fb3b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409660} {:answer/id #uuid "62a1a85b-a36c-54a7-b3bd-55a36f4cb315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409664} {:answer/id #uuid "77636ea6-fba2-560e-a69f-f3d2282f86f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409669} {:answer/id #uuid "81cdf51b-4eb6-557c-9452-11d65bc919bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409671} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe826b2-b0aa-57e0-a4ad-251af663d3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409671} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a00ccd6-15ad-566e-9909-ced57101fb7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a80ad4c-5529-5931-9d7c-574d40c6f47e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72748" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(B)=-7.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $2,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $2$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $2$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =2\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-2det(B) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2aef95ee-e7e9-5cac-b9ad-3b67a53db79f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e01723b-cc89-5e3f-b933-6517b2094770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409662} {:answer/id #uuid "91ff05b8-c923-5cbc-9974-2858e86d4877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409672} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b3cea5-79ed-5121-8909-27b79eb945a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409674} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a98397-80e5-5a87-b4ed-d166d96908c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409675} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7338ba-732f-5bfb-aded-530b4a5f8d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc16361c-3306-5054-a003-e31388cab6e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a9d752b-69e4-5f21-888f-3429a8443f7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42503" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=12.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2df25dd7-50b3-560b-9abe-b6b13654a237" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5903e6-2dfb-5988-91aa-d76353ccd959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409659} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3a3be9-dab5-5f7f-8e95-4eab3b47ad8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409665} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7bf72b-a934-51fa-b417-3e7f04a3f3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409666} {:answer/id #uuid "7190fbd3-e0c2-5f59-a1f5-3e49c40c46d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=2{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409668} {:answer/id #uuid "c8afe3f3-a5ef-55a7-b5dd-30e21d2e58fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409659} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a779adf-221a-5189-a14f-312c86e15c0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ac571f6-6f05-5f70-8bcf-34738f544556" :question/math-academy-id "q-43046" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e6fad07-ec42-5416-85eb-9ef2b0059cbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512abaff-433d-5e4a-864f-f1df10d56d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409663} {:answer/id #uuid "7a56edfe-4461-57d1-8804-22f3e005401a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409670} {:answer/id #uuid "f689939f-47fe-5c00-a125-9fe1fc1187ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409679} {:answer/id #uuid "face2d4d-2a2b-5eb7-8047-8fb6dbd54d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409680} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf8031f-e31a-5703-96b3-5a28bec971aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409682} :answer-field/id #uuid "98c176ba-da66-551c-9c0c-0c257e4d9459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "81c5993c-3d71-5869-bbf5-3e36b2df3a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72741" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(Y)=5.$ What is the value of $det(2Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2Y) & ={2}^{6}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =64\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =320.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d75809ee-199d-50e2-b002-2326810f1174" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409693}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409657}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424902} :activity/id #uuid "11b6f742-f965-5993-a06f-bca8dfba7485" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5631a190-3164-5c81-9c22-e6a70c44c09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263742} {:answer/id #uuid "74e1b546-dc5d-5c39-8615-b03158b816ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264014} {:answer/id #uuid "850ef7c7-1f4b-53c9-b078-25bda4009629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264145} {:answer/id #uuid "df7141db-3b66-5a7a-b89e-874dcb0bbee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264937} {:answer/id #uuid "e044d557-77d9-53d0-a554-77b04c1a4a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264014} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6cdcea-ffe0-5e06-a7c5-0284d3815f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e099506-9b8a-52ad-a7eb-ff6f42b6d002" :question/math-academy-id "q-130855" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=5$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(5) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0950983d-5529-5332-ba75-7636f5397a6b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0f29de-25aa-5107-8046-0dd732f9beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263097} {:answer/id #uuid "162deb0b-2730-571d-b037-7db47a3aea46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263209} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8f75e2-26ce-5967-ac8a-f06080423598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263259} {:answer/id #uuid "66de9e61-f678-5f09-92fa-20666c0a4a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263891} {:answer/id #uuid "f578aa5a-7ba5-5a91-8942-93f38512c9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263097} :answer-field/id #uuid "f00b119d-2bbd-5aff-88de-546820f3c832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da7f3dfc-3649-55d6-80af-942a6a83828d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130767" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(X)=28.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{3}{4},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{3}{4}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{3}{4}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{3}{4}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5a731d48-68b1-58c7-b15e-4a787d1f4b44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038f903c-07cf-5804-8ccf-c48c4437fe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326971} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5d0fbb-4c6d-516a-bbe1-bc9a4754768c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327766} {:answer/id #uuid "7edb508d-7daa-5a42-80ea-172f811c7a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328025} {:answer/id #uuid "cef987a3-28b5-50d6-be17-23ad82b7d421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328755} {:answer/id #uuid "dce0ee78-0312-5fae-84a0-aea62c27e409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328755} :answer-field/id #uuid "91964048-e6f4-5262-9e11-ecd0686eeeaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44ea0623-3f8f-525c-8344-8d911fbef23b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42504" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $3,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $3$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $3$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-3det(A) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5b098fcf-c1b8-5f75-8b3a-28dc83ab8997" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171230a8-065f-5312-91ff-166c6fb3df4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424893} {:answer/id #uuid "21bc26a0-57ad-5e62-a6e6-621e2ba24583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424894} {:answer/id #uuid "54d72f80-7a7b-5b93-a0ec-2d6edad08ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424895} {:answer/id #uuid "6c11e90f-184e-57bd-b3ed-1971d8fb82ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424896} {:answer/id #uuid "acde6755-f2db-5c6c-bdc1-ac03bdaa2931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424894} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75bea69-7db0-5820-97b1-b5dd95b8b845" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "200dace6-387b-586f-87ab-a5501c01cbe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-130851" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "796d84ed-30ec-5eef-9c9e-3ecd61007abc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17922ecf-87f8-5af9-872a-3d5dc6da26af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339339} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3f2587-b4ad-5bf0-81b7-1df62ffb7a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339686} {:answer/id #uuid "a21fdf0b-c950-5ec9-b67b-31a39827c266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340600} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1d7c41-28d1-55a3-9882-2c2b96500495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340714} {:answer/id #uuid "f3da6cbc-6c90-5ed2-9aad-88c3625d3d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341277} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded707a6-03c1-52cc-8868-8933afddd3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bce6072f-0fd1-50b6-a305-a94b72b85f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72740" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(Y)=-3.$ What is the value of $det(4Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(4Y) & ={4}^{3}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =64\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-192.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e253f5b-694c-5716-b2be-ec3f736b8398" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424901}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424892}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440490} :activity/id #uuid "f4aebbc3-f038-5073-b26d-b3a33a4479cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319beb45-da82-5928-83bf-d29bc52ed19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378659} {:answer/id #uuid "a259e746-f501-54ae-be55-fef569b47320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378660} {:answer/id #uuid "21d9eeeb-c150-51f2-9c34-5dfbb8074a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378661} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0902b3-371c-5ed1-bcca-bdf20d2b6da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378662} {:answer/id #uuid "6227f8a3-0c1e-5bad-a5c1-bba85528c3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378660} :answer-field/id #uuid "addc5023-5343-5d6e-94d4-459e9803d13f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbcc8a51-42ac-5cb8-a6bc-0647017d9ba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130858" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=14$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(14) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b56b3f3-0eed-5dd2-8e0f-81ffa9b89ff3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c504d34-49a8-5293-9654-9bb371251af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263118} {:answer/id #uuid "668e2da3-ac3d-5bec-b6b1-16504c2d81fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263886} {:answer/id #uuid "7573103e-73e5-56e6-832d-af6582780369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264021} {:answer/id #uuid "90344634-d8cd-51fb-85d7-7f08481a8bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236} {:answer/id #uuid "c168c56a-3c78-5c71-873d-48190da505aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236} :answer-field/id #uuid "37455212-8943-576e-a134-59b320488bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b71bc5d0-3557-578c-8a3d-25da59fcce16" :question/math-academy-id "q-130771" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-75.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{4}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-75) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1776f775-68f2-5dfe-9a78-5fcd889a28cc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b9b5a2-8dd1-5764-aa90-63de7756ab2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383218} {:answer/id #uuid "bea9ac5f-6965-5feb-9890-9a84bb8fa9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383219} {:answer/id #uuid "c930d2e4-f0f9-5e23-b593-fbc36215932d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383220} {:answer/id #uuid "dce12cc1-6fe9-5710-9501-662de6d6ea91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=-3{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383221} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5d3036-4719-5fd7-ba58-918a06722081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383220} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2936d46-0370-550d-9d6e-65ba694a9efd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "38a459ba-c9b0-57c4-8ac3-81669bb1022e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130848" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bc80a516-1dfa-5188-a931-81163ba5b5d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017c23cc-c363-5c6e-87b5-6e7c3ae5e977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440472} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4fda87-7e6e-55cf-a40d-16eb860dbe92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440474} {:answer/id #uuid "81b440ea-43a7-5db4-bd71-d49d81ed4b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440477} {:answer/id #uuid "b26eabf3-1144-53cd-932b-1525e01f62fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440479} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7979e9-0f01-5714-b611-2b2303ead7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440474} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1483cc5-4c9b-5aca-8098-5a910582cb85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba8abd45-3c22-5be5-a8a7-0f2c616dbcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130770" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(Y)=75.$ The matrix $Z$ is obtained from $Y$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{8}↔{R}_{9}$ and ${R}_{7}:={R}_{7}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(Z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{8}↔{R}_{9}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{7}:={R}_{7}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Z) & =-det(Y) \\\\  & =-(75) \\\\  & =-75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dddee882-e3d5-5fc5-bf4f-fc432c1be973" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a794be-72e1-5850-a9e7-4f09532bb4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440473} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9a3462-424a-5e23-93a1-21351e2c8529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440475} {:answer/id #uuid "3aad9ad4-b303-5b2b-9d26-6d2c4b559298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440476} {:answer/id #uuid "8a223201-44a6-5017-aa80-a127d44a8e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-147" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440478} {:answer/id #uuid "c00986c8-2ab1-5ba7-aed5-8ac51531c5fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-294" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440478} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad94b206-3ccb-5817-8ec1-05230e22359f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc497788-364d-5c29-8657-a4f1556e2704" :question/math-academy-id "q-72744" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(X)=-3.$ What is the value of $det(7X)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot X)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(X).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=7$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(7X) & ={7}^{2}\\cdot det(X) \\\\  & =49\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-147.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea78220d-d076-5d61-804b-70c264ba0078" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440489}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440471}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158042} :activity/id #uuid "3a40bda5-f823-5841-986e-d65c61c1a55f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373 :topic/id #uuid "ef92e0cb-7a97-5557-a9c4-4d553b2d60b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2832} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158605 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to find the images of simple objects in the plane under the action of nonlinear transformations.\n\nConsider the square region ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) and its boundary ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) in the ![](images/13/13377a2a85887e0a15df06f28ec7e6be35b98579a0865d0292a7f64da0d57b2e.svg)-plane, where ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/f2/f285f8016e64c5ab750d94dac618fe2c1a09ced6f5fce7bbaea85e7258eda2c5.svg)\n\nA sketch of this region, specified with a counterclockwise orientation, is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n     \n\nSince ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) is specified with an orientation, we refer to it as a **directed boundary.**\n\nNow, consider the transformation ![](images/0c/0cedb44f040d579cf90349a6891c23cb11a0cc35833979b96447815746831d35.svg) defined by\n\n![](images/a3/a3c2f6f5a3103e4fb023f9f433ce5fa33a215d23f5a33c8bf82b21b852e1236c.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n![](images/d5/d58f2d66646ec6255b8de257d9940476f78ea04fb65a8e0db6d19663399f0efa.svg)\n\nOur task is to find the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) under the action of ![](images/43/43a304a55ccd9c3f567bc9ca58aa2fb6b9aa0f8847f74a7c3dbbab3519729bb3.svg) \n\nFirst, note the following:\n\n- It's easy to check that ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) has no critical points in the *interior* of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) (we'll discuss why this matters shortly).\n\n- Since ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg)'s interior contains no critical points, we only need to find the image of its boundary ![](images/7d/7d7b652d219e930e1989bd99378c234b82ecbb8aa5eaec0574539267f3f70946.svg)\n\n- To find the image of ![](images/85/85bc8c8e3680298e08b6aecd3c4a66dcfb72f96ef1433d1bccb464af8311aee0.svg) we parametrize each *directed* edge and map them individually onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nLet's now map each directed edge of ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/37/3796e37203fb4ff84036acfaea5f94257c722a75b9b4896885548a54bd1e15ab.svg) where we have ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) and ![](images/23/233735103d4322c64d03fcb3c3c36b2968737ee86c5932190541afa9b0bf9c58.svg) Substituting ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/26/26d77a4baefb6a2d4a17264f5f8e7b9922e99cdedd62e6bfe2b08aba99ef0d17.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) can be parametrized by\n![](images/35/3567fd4fae25e9a36d2a48b68dec31311753e8cec3d13b0657db727124eaae3d.svg)\nWriting these parametric equations in the usual notation, we have\n![](images/4f/4fd68f3aee9087b1c0f8b86a57fae2035fa8527d5f0b74038d7c3339e8724457.svg)\nThese are parametric equations of a line segment. Moreover, the endpoints of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) are mapped to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane as follows:\n![](images/46/468ae20a0d581d780de7cb0af719f5a34e3141c44700b0e27353df12cb001bfe.svg)\nTherefore, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) is a line segment traversed from the point ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) to ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n![](images/13/132642571953ddbfc95c3f0c399cd3c612a2ba2a84a224939b6f40e7d22a074f.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/24/24d3419f793c33397116d086271facafc3e7dc955e696abc2edbd24b307d2d6e.svg) Here, we have ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) and ![](images/7c/7cebf80c26d37190278268fc56dc3b471c289f9f9b5112adac1210a972789142.svg) Substituting ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/41/41118e0a120a0a6df82567ce1925c2b4d21b8f6c50a1c8c927382361d4ab089a.svg)\nThis horizontal line segment is traversed from the point ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\n![](images/13/131644a2410e5127a69388f1bd1fced1e28698e7634b137fcd47f8b3f26121a3.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/c4/c4229f343b9b55013188ec3d2755c4f2b0517098764a1217d19ae440c0a93987.svg) Here, we have ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) and ![](images/80/8065c8e9bcaad0d0173e6a81ba9da342ceae55c13883b6ae9c9141c595b8bb7f.svg) (we write the inequality for ![](images/46/4620fefc72ebf96810ce9f348c9a728f392246ff87133b6d0e5549607fde421d.svg) in this way to show that ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) is traversed *from* the point where ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) *to* the point where ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg)).\n\nSubstituting ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/7e/7ea2885f9400b3908c7dfc98f22b094126c44142421b444b156e44f42e210762.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) can be parametrized by\n![](images/98/9838e3c4e7e9493167069fcdd079eb2137c6b163497626d42aa6e2283233996e.svg)\nWriting these in the usual notation, we have\n![](images/e4/e4ac0ebf10c9c6d9c69ce2ae73031bf89e3262351a9129fecd6e9225af2d9dd4.svg)\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) from the equation, we get\n![](images/d4/d488c4edbe9302b258749eb38ec9b104dcaab55c5eb63a88fcff9b00625b6b3b.svg)\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg) Moreover, the endpoints of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) are mapped to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane as follows:\n![](images/05/0537b85fecac45a4548b9907713fb5cbb1bafaeda9f7aa89f712c6e89cb739f8.svg)\nTherefore, the image of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) to ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg)\n\n![](images/bb/bb73b7c44c9fec45c3c3a632eed03db6f07db3ebbaff9e8e3941d22e78f478db.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/7a/7a8b22dcfbad030cb2ef985aa65b6051afcd75ee410eabdf7e88f931ccb53d81.svg) Here, we have ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg) and ![](images/2b/2b2a85d8c09127a2a022c9a63d1c522661922f621a2f3005c877b7c490ca359e.svg) Substituting ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/d6/d62c96cc3ca6e3993cfcf5d5ab2833922e0c6e80689ba8c8dc80e4186ad6cac4.svg) \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point ![](images/dc/dcc19bac01833ff23365244490e52b2722741da56e804e9a95b5b264f23216bf.svg) to the point ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\n![](images/7c/7c72a5f4341ab24df69d1375588abd655abbe8fec77ecd41e52501b602b7e3fb.png)\n\n      \n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully computed the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. \n\nIt's highly recommended that you redo this calculation yourself. Make sure you can construct the image of ![](images/13/13e2e6cea8ac23c623cd84a4d1d2ea58e3072ddcc5e8fc720cbe3e91ebff8fbe.svg) including its orientation (note that the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) is traversed *clockwise*)." :tutorial/id #uuid "5c011b18-998e-546d-a4cc-736249313138" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9449 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0183c504-9dca-5d57-b6b2-7fbd845c0c1b" :step/math-academy-id 179739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157028 :question/id #uuid "ec73a2b5-8f4a-5418-983d-b35013387844" :question/math-academy-id "e-15087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049035}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407211}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"} :step/id #uuid "57f1d55f-1700-5d32-8bb5-972acd409176" :step/math-academy-id 179740 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157247 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157027 :question/id #uuid "f35926af-6c5f-537f-ab22-ec2d62995267" :question/math-academy-id "e-15066"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416669}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "50421ebe-c890-5ba5-a32a-9a94c89dfcb8" :step/math-academy-id 179743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157248 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157025 :question/id #uuid "bb5306eb-02c5-52a3-b7b0-92610059b065" :question/math-academy-id "e-15064"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049508}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407209}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "a6c347f5-faac-5cfe-bf7c-5e603e73acbf" :step/math-academy-id 179747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158606 :tutorial/content "![](images/74/74e8a3822098768743be16f0af09d4365d424c83ea473eb56160f4c5f15c5531.png)\n\n        \n\nEarlier, we found that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( v(u^2+1), \\: u \\big) = \\big( u^2v+v, \\: u \\big) ,\n$$\n\nmaps the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. \n\nWe deduced the image of $\\Delta$ by mapping each edge of its boundary. For this method to work, we need to check that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ \n\nTo check for critical points, we start by computing the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor the partial derivatives of $x(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u^2v+v) = 2uv, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial v} =  \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u^2v+v) = u^2+1,\n$$\n\nand for the partial derivatives of $y(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u) = 1, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u) = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our partial derivatives into our expression for the Jacobian, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2uv & {u}^{2}+1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1+{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The Jacobian is negative everywhere in the $(u,v)$ plane. So, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n- Since the Jacobian is negative everywhere, the transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-reversing everywhere (note, for example, that $\\Delta$ and $D$ have opposite orientation).\n\nTo apply this method, we require that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ This method works even if the *boundary* of $\\Delta$ contains critical points. Let's explore this a little more." :tutorial/id #uuid "40f68814-34bc-5d92-b1f0-78ce6045757d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9450 :tutorial/title "The Significance of Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "f217b585-996f-5193-9dbe-40fa68d75ee1" :step/math-academy-id 179750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158607 :tutorial/content "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's again consider our square region $\\Delta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+uv) \\big).\n$$\n\nThe Jacobian matrix determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 2u+v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSetting the Jacobian equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n-2u^2 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ lie on the line $u = 0.$ This coincides with the side $S_4$ of our square.\n\n![](images/6e/6ea454e2a95bf5a9bef1d585c1620ff78a98586c2500293cdfd136c7562a5f83.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, since there are no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta,$ we can find its image in the usual way. However, the side $S_4$ may collapse to a single point. So, let's go ahead and finish this example." :tutorial/id #uuid "af247fbf-2e40-5486-964b-7b1effb2c06f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9451 :tutorial/title "Critical Points on the Boundary of the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "39d12d5d-e40b-5a0a-9a55-6c687ac4c3b5" :step/math-academy-id 179751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157026 :question/id #uuid "d08f8e35-c4e7-5eef-aca5-175585760433" :question/math-academy-id "e-15065"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436389}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"} :step/id #uuid "7ca8a633-49fc-5b82-89ef-24ced4348ee4" :step/math-academy-id 179752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158608 :tutorial/content "Until now, we've specified that our transformation $\\mathbf T$ should not contain critical points in the interior of the preimage $\\Delta.$ So let's briefly discuss what to do when $\\Delta$ contains critical points.\n\nOnce again, let's consider the region \n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin the $uv$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nWe now consider the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+v^2 \\big).\n$$\n\nLet's start by finding the critical points. First, we compute the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2(v+u)(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe critical points correspond to the solutions of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x,y)}{\\partial (u,v)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points are given by\n\n$$\n2(v+u)(v-u) = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u=\\pm v.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ that lie *inside* $\\Delta$ are located on the line $u = v.$\n\n![](images/af/af0cb392b9194a37c7f7e29455e066be52b28e11c8486edc8c992a82d3df625a.png)\n\n             \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ in this case, we divide $\\Delta$ into two regions $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ along the critical line (as shown below) and compute their images separately.\n\n![](images/96/965c24ef46882d40d4ff71b30b2ff60f891298a5dc48a267afc946f719e2b245.png)\n\n     \n\nLet's compute the images of these two regions:\n\n- Computing the image of $\\Delta_1$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_1)$ is a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_2)$ is an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,2),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_1)$ is a line segment traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/c7/c79e3dfcc15a6f5f7c77c59e99d67a29103e77a550a7c53a98b6868d786f5925.png)\n\n     \n\n- Similarly, computing the image of $\\Delta_2$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_2),$ a line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_3),$ an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_4),$ a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/eb/ebeae2b6425c15fb5670fc83dc64f07a2d407fd108d26c51d8f301d979732cdb.png)\n\n     \n\nSuperimposing the two results, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](images/28/28993d9cff3621f5b1633940c161aa528ef2b143bafca967cb55bdd9ffa4e1e0.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- As $(u,v)$ runs over $\\Delta$ once, its image $D$ is traced out twice.\n\n- The boundary of $D$ that runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2)$ is the image of the critical line $u=v,$ which is *not* a bounding curve of our original region $\\Delta.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8817e0ca-4c4f-5e82-b97a-2f4e6047eab4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9452 :tutorial/title "Critical Points Inside the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "658b558d-159e-5930-8d10-145b5fecb379" :step/math-academy-id 179755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158609 :tutorial/content "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n![](images/1b/1be386cd85ad48bcc75b4d292ebeeee0d8af0bf8df2297696b9384c448130bb3.svg)\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b766b50b744af71539daaf12c1e32e44f493ac71bbabb8b387c78bb8092886b8.svg)\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n![](images/f3/f3203536e63ab67521014fe9e8d623cf00535ae8d35adee9b6405253d532dcfc.svg)\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/55/55a8a36b0617400f963430eb1ba31ef20cbddeb4f09eaeaa8920fae39b4b145e.svg)\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{|det[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "07200f56-2408-51a2-9e3d-6b5d727ef5ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9453 :tutorial/title "The Jacobian as a Local Area Scale Factor"} :step/id #uuid "69d5e524-4c34-5148-a189-6502b171d912" :step/math-academy-id 179756}] :activity/title "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157488}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416675} :activity/id #uuid "23a36746-618b-5408-8fcd-c638b93c9f28" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373 :topic/id #uuid "ef92e0cb-7a97-5557-a9c4-4d553b2d60b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2832} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59020315-291c-59c3-9224-961256ce3b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416659} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdefaa7-89d2-5114-a2e1-0fce719d5db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/21/217244e03a7f3eb3d47d936a337de962d680d477ed944a75f7e74d426b53ff8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416660} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5ebdc7-9d12-52cf-8d15-a55e4c96ac2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a6/a60a2532e8cfea8044ddc5e52ef65a8fc83808fc8d35217130d243fe77e8c350.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416661} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcdde3a-a348-5fc5-968e-c1ee5b390866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2c/2c1d6e726da49ac8c398357ee00cbb53706a13a6baec25d35a3c594192d804eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416664} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5779ec-3893-54d8-8fd5-cb71c18cf754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/16/166fff724178c7285d6256c5b6f68c4181b66c246426e75759e196b2f2b3c0a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416659} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b03f791-a25e-5d56-8bc4-3d32145b2ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9cdc646-22ad-53d7-894a-78ecf8a59507" :question/math-academy-id "q-160633" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=uv.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u(0)) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2},\\,1v) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u(1)) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2},\\,0v) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "283e81ba-165c-5712-b058-a61afb12269f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31e82cf1-a965-5d27-8faf-45515b67a2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416657} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9762f5-d942-5b02-90e1-433937835992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416658} {:answer/id #uuid "758ca0b2-013b-5d8c-a6a5-3ac1ce65e6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,-1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416662} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d123e-9efa-5d77-821b-8c45d6d2e441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416663} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c04fe0-8935-5aff-a0db-580177fae6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416666} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47620cc-ff04-5478-975c-18645160652a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aedbf0c6-276b-509d-933d-5c28129e8de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-225139" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2{v}^{2}+u,\\,y=u-{v}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2{v}^{2}+u,\\,u-{v}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+u,\\,u-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2+u,\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2+u,\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2+t,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/2a/2ac0e0eac9e4633a8d29881782d8dfe93c4c594ca0f99912a98507822c97c4b4.png)"} :step/id #uuid "526a1df8-1606-5e36-8deb-549a340f512b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e97e49e-99a8-5167-95a0-4c3a2aa2b214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288228} {:answer/id #uuid "27c2c113-8813-50c2-98ea-35f094997ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288296} {:answer/id #uuid "370f9d77-be30-5124-88d9-5efd241282bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288404} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0618de-1ff4-56fe-a934-7f77d906a294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288667} {:answer/id #uuid "85690df4-4d70-55f9-838f-5da6f773888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288933} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb1a6c5-6f69-563f-88b4-ffb836efd328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "804dcc9f-4962-5beb-bc3d-f0bb08f62de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-225168" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v-2,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u+1-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u-1,\\,{u}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y={u}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={(x+1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(-1,-1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to (1+1-2,\\,{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1-2,\\,{0}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(-1,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$\n\n![](images/b1/b139c5b531191bc8aafbb14ad261989ef374503c63f54a567e695e287d4359be.png)"} :step/id #uuid "ba6262b9-b0fe-5e23-a4c4-b3dd83e994ec"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61988fc9-66c2-5bcc-83c1-08b33f8bfdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288717} {:answer/id #uuid "73ab294c-d61b-5d99-813d-6afefa5b3b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288837} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ad950e-b09a-59c9-8006-5f0ea759fde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289112} {:answer/id #uuid "a532b5c7-b1d3-5762-99e5-5ee19c94f559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289131} {:answer/id #uuid "ba18cd8c-2285-5486-8226-bfb496f3b81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289112} :answer-field/id #uuid "e191ff6e-214b-558e-9919-97349fa4da47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98c6216e-4e18-5967-be2c-62a3b8d14d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-225160" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=1-u+v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-u+v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-1+v) \\\\  & =(1-{v}^{2},\\,v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-{v}^{2},\\,y=v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=1-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=1-x\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2},1-1+0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}-{1}^{0},1-1+1)=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/99/995b106a25173b4e3667fd3fce04f499d7d4a549ae76414328f6e3c406ddd3c6.png)"} :step/id #uuid "bc6691b7-166a-5089-867e-8ee8a5dc5783" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416673}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaf80778-6e4d-52c2-a5db-4a2ef680da77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/19/19650cf966c3c455fa859877b4718c08aea8a343876e5817c625618098a84730.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389065} {:answer/id #uuid "c91635d8-70ba-5406-b235-c716cd8cb5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9d/9dc7559632cbecf450389f362ca478d40576ac9646489fda281519c48f49eb16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389066} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d4992f-3bd8-5933-9744-bd2784689f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/565227489ba6ae7a657a4b2f1e0133bc117e0e666557f58f59ec0272ad8c574e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389067} {:answer/id #uuid "d9925307-ecfb-5b78-8679-f58cb68b9e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068} {:answer/id #uuid "f94a3624-0a44-5d1c-a294-b6849858edb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/eb/eb6908aea50ad1c314efefdaaf922a8c637673a38f76ee8aadcbdc361f44ff78.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4309d7-1981-54e2-82f3-7e4f837d1ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e3c0bea-5d6e-5a25-bcb3-abdcd207f44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160632" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{0}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,1) &  \\\\  & =(1+{v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{1}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+1,\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,0) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png)"} :step/id #uuid "c4aa2659-822f-5c81-9961-6d81d19483c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416671}}] :activity/title "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416656}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436392} :activity/id #uuid "5d1b8939-e94e-5773-a283-f5d685750aa5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373 :topic/id #uuid "ef92e0cb-7a97-5557-a9c4-4d553b2d60b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2832} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2082dc17-8677-5072-8728-eb1279bccdcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ee/eec7dbae504f01dda802a07eb200fcf9a1823ffd3a71665b87936b24e7e5525a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436378} {:answer/id #uuid "69783711-c08d-571c-86a6-8b131065e28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/868f4a5fc5f2e2d30dc5d1a693cb8df7bd6cbdad90483d806a3c08afc521d1ff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436381} {:answer/id #uuid "816f6705-f146-5bfb-a96a-c00de5846b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/72/7215170dcbc618f7a006cc4aedd65bdc36dd8bccd708bbe9784dcbfc4974efea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436384} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba99d86-24e8-5ca6-94cd-b5ea2ff49d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3b/3b06791c21bcb41f654c1d01f8e4b9cb58df36cf76615ce0b41a0245835c38d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436385} {:answer/id #uuid "afc4090a-a50d-5cfd-b9cf-41e1abf26da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cc/ccd7576940650b3c1ba7a0b0c404b2dc39d287261d77f556f63b8166496c69df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436387} :answer-field/id #uuid "d96343de-f671-5ac9-bcd5-9b8c7a8dc39b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "015f5e64-8ccf-5a6a-8fbf-8ce2c8d20eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-160631" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+0,\\,-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1+v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(2,-1)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/cc/ccd7576940650b3c1ba7a0b0c404b2dc39d287261d77f556f63b8166496c69df.png)"} :step/id #uuid "01e6ff8f-00df-5949-ac6e-0410dc19e1c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd9f9d6-0406-5b60-bf3c-c7fb79ebaf40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/72/72e271d6a2282fdf7cdf852308f569de848b8bdc71935e1487969f9ea7ac3eb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373904} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c07d75-8dc0-5472-b4ee-118ce7ee6da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e3/e3cfcf4c6cd7bcc8298a7e3d8c76f4add3f61689cecc1dacb9fa8a48db5a042b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373905} {:answer/id #uuid "4157d539-f77d-5f54-8554-c73457ce6de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/64/648fa48df1bd648d9fbf48efc966698228416ad9bf3a0f69045031e955601d61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373906} {:answer/id #uuid "c5415f6b-51be-511e-8dcf-7bc985ea09e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3b/3ba341827d95d261f8def4462a11282051b3605795459343441f9f35284faf33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373907} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8fcb79-14f8-54fd-8a6b-4be84dab14e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0f/0fd03370c63540a8dc9336305bbf917239f02afce2f73371715d6489eee31c9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373906} :answer-field/id #uuid "515b035c-875d-5a93-a2bf-bdd77d9642fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e4de9b2d-c42a-5fe3-94b3-1b2caac795c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160636" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2},\\,y={u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u({0}^{2}),\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1{v}^{2},\\,{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u({1}^{2}),\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0{v}^{2},\\,{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/64/648fa48df1bd648d9fbf48efc966698228416ad9bf3a0f69045031e955601d61.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1a287a6a-b990-525b-8eed-0161adc076ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4e018a-bce1-5b10-a192-2b9d54af71ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436379} {:answer/id #uuid "57862c3c-92cb-58ef-989f-3796b1f3d3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436380} {:answer/id #uuid "809bfe03-d49c-5afa-b88d-848b85922201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436382} {:answer/id #uuid "8166063a-dc78-5f3f-8bf7-0c141a4e8d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436383} {:answer/id #uuid "96d545bf-0741-5454-b677-38f6a4355e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436383} :answer-field/id #uuid "220de041-8a77-59e5-81bf-784ea13caa4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13842938-0c5a-56c1-b7e1-75af1a627153" :question/math-academy-id "q-160610" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v,\\,u-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\,u-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment with endpoints $(1,0)$ and $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/4c/4cda505c17437f64f8ab90dcccca1f55f087af77be6dee0e7c81dbd372760664.png)"} :step/id #uuid "8a728ed1-bdb3-5d79-95ad-38502010151b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06977182-5497-5c11-a57f-add4aeca5aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288084} {:answer/id #uuid "2ece2530-2ca7-5ec1-a8cf-c55ac6d46052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288347} {:answer/id #uuid "8539bd62-399f-5793-bd3f-e4de85fa29cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288928} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d93a8c-2868-5548-992e-86797465c0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289208} {:answer/id #uuid "bdbf2fce-7275-51db-ae2f-706d92628bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288347} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf66fd9-3498-5637-8fc1-454524321dda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13002a7e-2ad6-501a-b8db-7a8abc685d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-225122" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}(v+6u),\\,y=\\frac{{v}^{3}-2u}{v+2}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(v+6u),\\,\\frac{{v}^{3}-2u}{v+2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(0+6u),\\,\\frac{{0}^{3}-2u}{0+2}) \\\\  & =(2u,-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/f0/f068883d1318acdafba39c74bc1279f6cafe803437a9b79249999df461bac8ee.png)"} :step/id #uuid "c2c63c2f-25ab-55d2-98c6-3bc6cfeeba08"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc68d80-39d1-5f0f-933c-89bd1b4b4d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373897} {:answer/id #uuid "b20916f4-2125-5e54-be30-7b22ca6d6f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373898} {:answer/id #uuid "f69006ab-7ec1-5771-bd33-a19fe8da912c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373899} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fa41a-6316-5747-87cf-e13860d909ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373900} {:answer/id #uuid "732254b6-7d89-598e-b5b3-79f480fa4f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373897} :answer-field/id #uuid "419acef2-b0e8-560d-bfb9-5d30aad77e63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ebd6f32-1c51-561f-a98e-5d832c9928ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160611" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}+1,\\,-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}+1,\\,y=-v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+1,\\,y=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=(-y)^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=x-1\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}+{0}^{2},-0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}+{1}^{2},-1)=(2,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$\n\n![](images/ad/ade48b3f14ed83460a5d818f744103edd75f8394a3f3396a0ff30364bda36c9b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "fd5f8cf2-cf21-51d5-98dd-cd133c6f5651" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436390}}] :activity/title "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436377}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577} :activity/id #uuid "37fac92b-9502-52a2-8069-399fccbd8a62" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"} :step/id #uuid "a6ab275a-61df-5648-811a-176fda344fe5" :step/math-academy-id 30861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479104}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"} :step/id #uuid "8627eaba-93e3-51d5-b350-72ee5af3e635" :step/math-academy-id 30862 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479133}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "60b2ea1e-4b9a-5557-99ae-a83016e785a6" :step/math-academy-id 30863 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166588 :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "26ddca41-ae16-5395-ae11-41c4d70303c6" :step/math-academy-id 30864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168061 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for single variable functions $f=f(x)$ and $x=x(t)$ states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nLet's now suppose that $f = f(x,y),$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$ are single-variable functions. How do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d t}$ in this case?\n\nThe functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are known as **intermediate variables.** They are independent variables for the function $f(x,y),$ but are themselves dependent on the variable $t.$\n\nOne way to consider how $f(x,y)$ depends on $t$ is to draw a tree diagram.\n\nStarting with $f(x,y),$ we draw a branch for each intermediate variable. Then, we draw a branch for both $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ which are both dependent on the variable $t.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdc47e038ecaba62ae612a0ac0bc07067af232353fc4fc169bf4be57b3abbd1f.png)\n\nWe then label each branch with the derivative describing how the function changes due to a change in the variable. \n\n![](images/ec/ece6012e358b5b6920395df88acd8d7037a7b15bed1add1220365ba72bd24cbd.png)\n\nThen, traversing each branch, we compute the product of the derivatives along each branch and place the result at the end of the branch.\n\n![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nFinally, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we sum the results for each branch:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}\n = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThis result is called the **multivariable chain rule**." :tutorial/id #uuid "80ad8a26-7d43-52d6-b0ba-e1d04283e5ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4957 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "03fe551f-81c6-5c42-9baf-d6d12895ab47" :step/math-academy-id 30893 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168062 :tutorial/content "How do we apply the multivariable chain rule if the intermediate variables are also dependent on two variables? \n\nFor example, suppose we have\n\n$$\nf = f(x,y), \\qquad x=g(s,t), \\qquad y=h(s,t).\n$$\n\nHow do we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}?$ \n\nFirst, let's construct a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/ca/ca524eda2e6be3354fcb72bed52fcaf22257073c3228d74850f8226f9ef0af6f.png)\n\nNow, since we're interested in $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s},$ we highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $s:$\n\n![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nIf we keep $t$ fixed and differentiate $f$ with respect to $s,$ then according to the chain rule for two intermediate variables and one independent variable, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we wanted to compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t},$ we would highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nKeeping $s$ fixed and differentiating $f$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58d189b1-31fb-5b1b-bc4c-cff6be5e2f7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4958 :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "2ffafece-1c0b-50f7-a3b1-e1b2d1bada36" :step/math-academy-id 30894 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168063 :tutorial/content "We can generalize the chain rule to a multivariable function containing an arbitrary number of intermediate and independent variables.\n\nConsider the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf = f(x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_m)\n$$\n\nwith $m$ independent variables and, for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots, n\\},$ the variable \n\n$$\nx_i = g_i(t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_n)\n$$\n\nis a differentiable function of $n$ independent variables. Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t_j} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_1}{\\partial t_j} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_2}{\\partial t_j} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_m}{\\partial t_j}\n$$\n\nfor $j \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots, n\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ba7d2a4-05a6-5e62-8035-2610239633c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4959 :tutorial/title "The Generalized Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "9731a04f-c2d1-5342-b940-0313b71ca1eb" :step/math-academy-id 30895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168088 :tutorial/content "There is a special case of the multivariable chain rule when $f=f(x,y)$ and $y=g(x).$ \n\nNotice that $x$ is an independent variable here. Therefore, the chain rule becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}}_{⏟}}_{1}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44effd03-02e7-5eb1-8732-ee1522afa147" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5184 :tutorial/title "A Special Case of the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "fdc61eff-9eab-552e-9092-f4040bdff21a" :step/math-academy-id 32539 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166978}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420998} :activity/id #uuid "844676a2-0a90-5f03-a7c1-22515b371e49" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a19a158-cb30-5c9e-9cf9-891ab114ad36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(2t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355012} {:answer/id #uuid "392f1f1c-1dad-5e72-b1e4-40cf201a767c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}t(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355179} {:answer/id #uuid "869f5f73-0c65-5003-ade6-8eeef3e8b4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355855} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f0fe58-16c4-5d79-ab11-4893e9ff089a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(s-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356369} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ace28-41b6-5a2e-8b64-9dee698d22fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314b903-87e4-5c8c-a8db-703d520d008b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1e3cf4a-155f-587d-ba03-cc737759407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94448" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y,$ where $x=2st$ and $y={s}^{2}t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2st)=4st, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(4st)(2t)+(-4)(2st) \\\\  & =8s{t}^{2}-8st \\\\  & =8st(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cf7698e-82a3-5979-93cb-d2edffbb99d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9a8991-24fd-50f9-8941-a329e3133c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+10{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321230} {:answer/id #uuid "84b790fb-dce2-5ea5-9fbd-fb66967b78c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-20s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322195} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f027a-f67b-5d1f-b407-9ee3b85b3667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322598} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bfc905-fde6-54a5-83ad-c5fa1e325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} {:answer/id #uuid "f675ee7a-8c5d-5a9a-90cb-d0e4dd971fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-15s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1de9ae-38d9-5d66-a275-9fc76c319cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bd5b5db6-0961-5b1c-8f50-24c2f4348bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37132" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-5yz,$ where $x=st,$ $y={s}^{3}$ and $z=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-5z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)({s}^{3})=2{s}^{4}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(st)^{2}-5(s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-5{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2{s}^{4}t)\\cdot t+({s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3{s}^{2}+(-5{s}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-15{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a9db776-142d-5ace-9b62-834d4e4b6a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021a846a-8825-55bf-a7b3-1dc5c5c5ff19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281709} {:answer/id #uuid "49b2d0a3-8640-5928-a7d2-f8a14102a68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a1e1c7-9e8a-5f17-9500-4d7507d44b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283715} {:answer/id #uuid "efd819c9-60d2-5826-9266-3dba4a49aaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283779} {:answer/id #uuid "f5de75cd-c44e-5baf-9f07-d98f3333edab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} :answer-field/id #uuid "d658fcd0-7d37-5a87-a5b6-42706ffef583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdba7da9-8893-51f6-a38d-eccc847b176c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170670" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ef0c797-03b2-58eb-8a37-83236f476fe8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be18679-efe0-59e6-80ac-db92f8d96a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9edd72-2523-57c6-9665-681f1ed475c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358439} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcdf56c-f98e-5632-8b37-69db930425d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b3c97-4572-526c-8afd-d0a1d5757af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359141} {:answer/id #uuid "b33efd16-e5a4-5396-8988-6df4afae8ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f643ac2-8342-50cb-9136-e026a1d69059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfb1f74a-0323-5d9e-a2f6-877356ed98f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95118" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+yx$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7bfbd495-1860-54a3-8d60-35f838cfca25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17573a89-2ada-5cfd-8592-1b89b18d61f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420990} {:answer/id #uuid "3856cad9-d2c1-55a4-adf6-784ae35a6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420991} {:answer/id #uuid "5c54b913-d15a-53bc-b2c6-f71477530f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420992} {:answer/id #uuid "ae277bb1-236f-56f1-8a0c-7fd47295108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} {:answer/id #uuid "d87585d5-b5cd-5fbc-b58c-a3d2c45f4e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} :answer-field/id #uuid "f118ee24-10a3-5bda-8238-4be0365cace0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f22c7a35-af74-59c8-b71b-cae8347ea97d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37206" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+{z}^{2},$ where $x={s}^{2}+{t}^{2},$ $y=\\frac{s}{t}$ and $z=s{t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+{c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}s{t}^{4},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{s}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{s}{t})\\cdot 2s+({s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2})\\cdot {t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{t}+\\frac{{s}^{2}}{t}+t+st+st+2s{t}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+t+2st+2s{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=1+2+2=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "926bef20-92d0-5cd4-928e-9a8dd40763b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420997}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420989}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030} :activity/id #uuid "f74c4548-dfc9-5c4c-bb83-8b7024f32b7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130375 :tutorial/content "Double integrals have properties that are similar to the properties of definite integrals of single-variable functions:\n\n- **Linearity of Double Integrals.** Suppose that  $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ are continuous functions on a domain $D$ and that $\\alpha, \\beta$ are constants. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\alpha f(x,y) + \\beta g(x,y)\\right] \\ \\textrm d A = \\alpha\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A + \\beta\\iint\\limits_{D} g(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A.\n$$\n\n- **The Double Integral of Unity.** The integral of $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D).\n$$\n\nOther properties are more specific to double integrals. \n\n- **The Multiplicative Property of Double Integrals.** If $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and $g(y)$ is continuous on $[c,d]$ then \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x)g(y) \\ \\textrm d A = \\left(\\int_a^b f(x)  \\ \\textrm d x\\right)\\left( \\int_c^d g(y) \\ \\textrm d y\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6158466e-1222-52a1-88ad-028b5c5832c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3860 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "15730ddd-c579-5f89-bff8-85f42db15b97" :step/math-academy-id 22622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128989 :question/id #uuid "53185b25-c690-500a-9a82-c18b37668e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-6106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec44686a-601b-52b3-81df-e57e6708fb93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445484}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Multiplicative Property"} :step/id #uuid "2526c89c-75e6-5851-84b4-83ae4eaabc04" :step/math-academy-id 22623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130376 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the multiplicative property when the integrand contains only one of the variables. \n\nFor example, suppose that $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$ Then, we have the general result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(f(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{c}^{d}1\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)(d-c) \\\\  & =(d-c){\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nBy the same reasoning, we also have \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}A = (b-a) \\int_c^d g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3acdc936-b0cf-5aca-a57a-00bdc558bb88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3861 :tutorial/title "Applying the Multiplicative Property when the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "12e3fe87-21fe-5661-a2ed-48880eba8e48" :step/math-academy-id 22624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128990 :question/id #uuid "a5567cb9-1c16-56a6-b0e8-759edf693a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6107"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445488}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e720a516-990b-5dfb-b236-aca8fdfa01d1" :step/math-academy-id 22625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128991 :question/id #uuid "0cb8fa8a-5be1-5f53-bfde-5acf325f6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-6108"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8059a197-e9fb-5306-8eed-62c0cea88f33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496248}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Additivity Over a Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "f422ea91-80bb-5373-9f64-444e0646074d" :step/math-academy-id 22627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130397 :tutorial/content "Remember that the definite integral of a single-variable function can be split up over adjacent subintervals. That is to say,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_b^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nIn the same way, the definite integral of a two-variable function can be split up over adjacent sub-domains.\n\nSuppose that a domain $D$ is the union of two adjacent sub-domains $D_1$ and $D_2$ (meaning that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are non-overlapping regions that could share a common border). Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThis property can be generalized if $D$ is split into a finite number $n$ of sub-domains (where $n>2$):\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\cdots + \\iint\\limits_{D_n}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bc4e2a2c-afd3-57e1-ac95-737c568153b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4258 :tutorial/title "Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "5249980b-32b5-5965-880e-a7948ef2b5f3" :step/math-academy-id 25886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129188 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129007 :question/id #uuid "1c227b12-d40b-53a8-b568-b7bd380acbee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7023"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8505beff-75b8-57bf-9312-e0fcf32f44fd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496250}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "bca38320-513b-5017-88bb-f5d1a8c6013e" :step/math-academy-id 25887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130398 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're integrating a single-variable function over a region $R.$ The definite integral of $f(x)$ over $R$ is not affected by whether the region $R$ is open or closed.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x) = 1,$ and define the regions $R = [0,1]$ and $R' = (0,1).$ Integrating $f(x)$ over $R$ and $R'$ gives the same result:\n\n$$\n\\int_R f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{R'} f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{0}^1 \\,\\textrm d x = 1\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the rectangles formed by $f(x),$ namely $[0, 1]\\times [0,1]$ and $(0, 1)\\times (0,1),$ have the same area. \n\nWe have a similar property for double integrals too. We can either include or exclude the boundaries of the integration regions without affecting the result. \n\nFor example, the double integral of $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ over both $R=[0,1] \\times [0,1]$ and over $R'=(0,1) \\times (0,1)$ will give the same result:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large R} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R'} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the *volumes* of the shapes formed by $f(x,y)$ over the regions $R$ and $R'$ are the same.\n\nMoreover, we'd get the same result if we were to integrate $f(x,y)$ over any of the following regions:\n\n$$\n(0,1] \\times [0,1], \\quad [0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad (0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to double-integrals over non-rectangular boundaries, too." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f51d452-a229-5d76-885e-0fe84f787707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4311 :tutorial/title "Open and Closed Regions of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "45710b0f-af68-5a20-b9ce-c5ecc560f4e5" :step/math-academy-id 26202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034}}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129345}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655} :activity/id #uuid "abdc4a65-40f3-528e-9141-f38a1d8b8f37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc8e0ee-42d1-5e33-ab3e-d149ce209193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128574} {:answer/id #uuid "216a895e-32d8-5d87-938d-db65b2d1bddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419645} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5a35ab-9584-5250-9b05-bab514871947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19-20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419646} {:answer/id #uuid "c482d14a-968e-5fba-92dc-75544ab448dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20-19e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} {:answer/id #uuid "c6534224-e681-59ea-874f-2267428a83f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16+20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} :answer-field/id #uuid "5432046c-908f-53bb-aa5e-d0f6af32c211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9bb5733-5b86-5301-9267-6ae190810785" :question/math-academy-id "q-71994" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{x}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(3-2){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{2}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(e-1)[{y}^{3}]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =(e-1)({3}^{3}-{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =19(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A=1-19(e-1)=20-19e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b0d3e0f-96a8-599d-92f9-04874e8f5b73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fb41e7-e3b3-596b-9de2-56afe6dd5f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9ef82d-4456-5d25-ad26-90ed8d7f4c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346295} {:answer/id #uuid "705f8690-2e07-5932-b07c-8089792360e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{T}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346333} {:answer/id #uuid "9666eb71-130a-59fc-8098-2ba78743d703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346666} {:answer/id #uuid "c006f721-3d51-5d16-a6a8-1b8222268571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}(x+{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef450e5-1471-5ba8-8652-38a2e4337160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e01cd29-3b44-5c08-bc75-551f046b44dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80452" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed0be2aca8d43d733e91f3a8feaaca0649957dcbd516a8218b1e7dea136170d8.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ {x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}0\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b57250fe-0a51-501d-8a2f-9f787d4986a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168581f2-f17c-5d44-a38c-f447560aea9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d9732d-dbc5-596f-ab65-73c4ce86f71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419649} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b906bf-abf8-5f7b-aa2d-46eb0fb79934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419650} {:answer/id #uuid "da997437-2a62-59ee-9431-4e9d511ef420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419651} {:answer/id #uuid "f67323c5-5cdb-58d8-a3cb-89a513553c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e876f8-837f-5088-b5c0-91f0a9d62487" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a49df285-b000-551a-958c-a18cbfff682c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80659" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=-3,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=5,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =-2\\cdot \\,\\,\\,{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2\\cdot ({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-3+5) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f337f9e2-418d-5c24-9790-11f1621a546e"}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419643}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496386} :activity/id #uuid "95d8bbec-04bf-5628-a449-ebbd525dd776" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424c1725-f6be-557b-8ba1-de6cd2c48a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382464} {:answer/id #uuid "ad38cb99-0e64-5f96-baca-e7d0549cba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} {:answer/id #uuid "1e089d84-f069-5f2c-83fa-ebc8d830878a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382466} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fc8d95-fed9-543b-84f8-e46cb703d09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382467} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1aadd1-ba01-5e68-a02a-c2ed4e7c1f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86e0dfb-0f1e-5397-8c0c-93b6e0bbf141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "265058e2-39b1-59f9-b955-fd7951910713" :question/math-academy-id "q-81253" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\pi , \\\\ x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-1,1)\\times (0,\\pi )$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,2)\\times (0,\\pi ).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi }+[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0)+(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5d158e0b-364f-57cd-9861-9ba595b098dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bb7e4f-4f89-5de1-95f7-da1922b3e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495583} {:answer/id #uuid "4abebdbc-99ae-50c0-a512-0a23de882c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495682} {:answer/id #uuid "714c7021-6f5d-5cb3-9a9a-f76210471846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495759} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e8c850-fb04-565b-82b5-6a4a220870b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495906} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b29483-e8e2-58c0-b501-11ed5e1de262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495970} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f162e99-d897-57bf-9c7b-c3f9fc5e3dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "426d96cd-8cff-562b-8539-4a443da3b8d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80668" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1, \\\\ 2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,1<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(0,1)\\times (0,1)$ and ${R}_{2}=(0,1)\\times (1,2).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-0){\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+({x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1})\\cdot ({e}^{y}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1\\cdot ({e}^{2}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & =1+{e}^{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6aea6f9b-1c3a-5b35-93ee-7c684d8d6000" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a89715-7a54-5300-819b-fa080b061464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382450} {:answer/id #uuid "56fb164e-39a3-5e17-815d-ec81eaeb5642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382451} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cb3d91-b5e5-5157-8a43-6dd0b3fbf045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382452} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf0027f-33d6-5dec-9265-dd1640066c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382453} {:answer/id #uuid "5f806e22-cdeb-5e59-8750-426e910009d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ffc185-1273-5e29-8ff4-7540cee84426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9713fa5-57af-5823-80a4-91a27fbe3a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-81244" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-3,0]\\times [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(4-0){\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(0-(-3)){\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third integral is the double integral of unity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(R)=3\\cdot 4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A=36-24+12=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7eca13c9-165d-5443-bea1-7c1c3600f648" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29cb87ec-7b62-54a3-9b91-6761bf4ff971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495602} {:answer/id #uuid "32a207e8-72fc-5597-b6e1-52b672dc0edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495627} {:answer/id #uuid "64babd30-e58f-5686-b182-18182e671ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495734} {:answer/id #uuid "79a8bba4-a404-5ef1-853e-20b00d2df235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495772} {:answer/id #uuid "ec45ef3b-c17c-5ae2-8f29-beba53a099ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495772} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cf7a13-0b14-5e86-98cd-0f8088bad90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4896f06f-886c-5ec8-9b07-a29fce8c9f73" :question/math-academy-id "q-130021" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=2,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=7,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}\\frac{1}{3}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}5f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(7+2) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4b78fd8-e01d-5b99-936b-9991b9a39b49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd94ac7-3795-5961-a7c1-57dd5b247105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495685} {:answer/id #uuid "683d5a4b-306a-5d43-8f8c-20fb207ba1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495740} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9d4d09-68ab-514c-8c81-4e473a25d36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}x\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495863} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b9981d-d744-5f2e-820e-320f4ec86a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495876} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b195e5-7146-58b0-bd21-eaaacb148632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495876} :answer-field/id #uuid "89e973d3-c94c-5288-b1d4-40bdaaeee25a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fc1b49a-d775-540a-9853-9d70731cf1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80656" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c6e4deccce395f5bdc8aadef8cd2c712804235c340ccae174f2067211a5a49c.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ {x}^{2}+y, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eae75d8c-b827-5748-96f3-9fc94f7fde49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496356}}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495503}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120695} :activity/id #uuid "ad4d6410-a365-5f77-a4b1-3e2d134d5a3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638 :topic/id #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" :topic/math-academy-id 1864} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121042 :tutorial/content "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n![](images/05/0593ca6a838b66c4623e8749eb5236d6259b1f9bf36ed0d68b83c49781076341.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n![](images/f3/f355faa0c477775f2a1cbb3522d0743cdbc9649a5ee9c55b579b1fac9ae2a661.svg)\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector ![](images/78/78357fcf51c9cd6b7873f5bc35fcc214cb3790bcec00ee69d0932f15aacd39cb.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n![](images/82/82d4f494936d23e9b697aa4518edf68f7ea62e504d9d2576a6d13971acaf1c96.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bb4f07e6-7c2f-5fe2-b96b-1bb37603981a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3208 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "55354f89-d587-5329-9788-b198ebb218a3" :step/math-academy-id 17513 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119631 :question/id #uuid "0a0ea798-5125-5d0e-a755-96bf9af5f543" :question/math-academy-id "e-4823"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4f1675-9146-5279-9b64-07a63a9bed99" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430732}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative To a Given Basis"} :step/id #uuid "b2734184-6a42-50f6-9a19-566236b06025" :step/math-academy-id 17514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119632 :question/id #uuid "6c7237cd-c655-575f-83ac-7a0891c336b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4824"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "841f498b-f737-5086-8f79-44f1a143bebb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430731}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "94aa1ebf-d415-58aa-a0dc-17e8201c1669" :step/math-academy-id 17515 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119633 :question/id #uuid "90f07e38-405a-5f79-8501-0b4dfb8fa4c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4825"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6a6ea8-6100-5224-b75f-b87c037fafbb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455505}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing More Than Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "34b75a42-9505-5418-a27f-2b9aade2bc07" :step/math-academy-id 17516 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121066 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a set of vectors\n![](images/0d/0d78e1b2d01003885882f19b3856278e5024719f26f33594fd071fbd73273b71.svg)\n\nNotice that the set $\\mathcal{S}$ is linearly independent since the vector equation $x_1\\mathbf{e}_1+x_2\\mathbf{e}_2+x_3\\mathbf{e}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ leads to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution. \n\nAlso, notice that any vector in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{e}_1,$ $\\mathbf{e}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{e}_3.$ For instance,\n![](images/ef/ef28dd8cbad6d256b9fa82c487263af3327ce630839cfcd1c7433417d0e0ae73.svg)\n\nIn particular, the equation above tells us that \n![](images/72/724f42afb534edbde7588c5508a4bf8de6d3cd068bc2989da7c7207b1bf7a1ba.svg)\nthat is to say, the numbers $\\color{red}-2,$ $\\color{red}0,$ $\\color{red}5$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nThe basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is somewhat special, since the coordinates of a vector relative to this basis coincide with the components of the vector. We refer to $\\mathcal{S}$ as the **standard basis**. Remember that in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3$ we even have special notations for the vectors of the standard basis:\n![](images/ae/aee5dd3226ebe950defc8b873399a884e0679ce701a3a55354ef01960e029f8e.svg)\n\n![](images/dd/dd96e0b70173adf8983feb4975c351ab3e417c34ed4a6a944bc391e33db4c8e9.png)\n\nIn general, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n![](images/a8/a8e4b648898c0dc036e228fb84945833dc40c053beff034596e098c4dd077e23.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "54e7c1fd-c7bf-5c77-bcc5-eb8d93a11582" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3969 :tutorial/title "The Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9053925c-8744-5312-b890-f5e4a1a8d2e8" :step/math-academy-id 23574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119674 :question/id #uuid "a79126f2-e26c-5929-93d8-825d85b9a438" :question/math-academy-id "e-6282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2926764-dfbc-5e4e-a6b0-db24a7298a64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409819}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "79095f38-ffd3-565b-a534-f136c7646422" :step/math-academy-id 23612 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120697}}] :activity/title "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120054}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416947} :activity/id #uuid "e9bd4727-090c-5259-8cb9-53f91a718d53" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638 :topic/id #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" :topic/math-academy-id 1864} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b2da92-80e1-5a61-a19c-e0402c5db36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416936} {:answer/id #uuid "94ec8a9d-591d-59ec-b9c5-a655500ebc04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416937} {:answer/id #uuid "c791cf81-feb5-59a1-bf25-b0be74c138f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416940} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdbeb50-bd46-5e6a-a5d9-d2fa3f1bb293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416942} {:answer/id #uuid "f2716f4b-b756-5daf-b7ff-420d367fb480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416936} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd25317-6ae7-5ed2-b2f8-06a74441129a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1098aac8-90b3-5ba0-b447-7ead2947d568" :question/math-academy-id "q-35247" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-4(5)=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+5=9.$"} :step/id #uuid "52d64f85-27e5-5e6b-8c92-f165572fe095" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f5fc79-efcf-5beb-8f44-6c5bbee2e205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416934} {:answer/id #uuid "460c227b-da50-5240-a61b-5d7158cbefb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416935} {:answer/id #uuid "b96e7099-61f5-552d-a37f-75ea4d0acd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416938} {:answer/id #uuid "c28d8b2c-0794-5b52-be1a-1a5068c24206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416939} {:answer/id #uuid "ca59db9a-208c-56e6-9d14-d390aecd3368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416935} :answer-field/id #uuid "c652bfa5-8e6a-53fc-ad6f-da352c6f194d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93dd069a-4710-5057-b8fe-736d3ab4c0dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75844" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-4)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "598de137-99ae-5ba2-ac49-2dd82ea7ee2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0470cd3b-2bcb-575e-9def-d43004406207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409787} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e208c3-ba1a-51ec-b367-c2784fabe06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409800} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba42a7-b00b-5980-9f89-48160c1eb49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409801} {:answer/id #uuid "d13c159c-f442-5933-a821-d94880c3ba32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409806} {:answer/id #uuid "eec553dd-6fd7-5208-9292-850213e9c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409801} :answer-field/id #uuid "a79bee7d-12b6-5017-ae13-ca00a861bd24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec45397c-5ced-5a47-b9a9-86c6aa1d1bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76073" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ \\,\\,-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\,\\,0=5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the third equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6074dbdf-0c2f-509e-aa43-e4c385bf7861"}] :activity/title "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416933}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426858} :activity/id #uuid "7b9fccdb-a888-5b80-9f71-5e4ef95dcf8c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638 :topic/id #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" :topic/math-academy-id 1864} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e83f3a8-e73e-5dcd-ac90-e7fbd7a63237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426844} {:answer/id #uuid "1298c82b-5f34-5b3d-9a2e-19a4db3c3122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426845} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2c222c-23e3-58cc-87ec-9f7920cb1f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426847} {:answer/id #uuid "b92f0488-96a7-580a-9581-5603eab3169c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426851} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9d08f1-fc9e-5876-bc5a-62f5443e92fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426845} :answer-field/id #uuid "3659e401-201b-5665-a817-8016820a5661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "973ba0d1-71e0-5bd1-b707-8f3f23076709" :question/math-academy-id "q-34761" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 2 & 16 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 2 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 12{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=16 \\\\ {x}_{3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second  equation yields\n\n$$\n12{x}_{2}+2(2)=16\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n Finally,  by substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+4(1)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+1+2=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23a4ea36-89a6-56d3-bf56-d577c8099f1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c86a8e6-6f85-5aa5-ae6d-29029bdec356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245272} {:answer/id #uuid "9aad1bac-86db-5804-9c07-c985acb44877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245681} {:answer/id #uuid "a44bbdac-4145-547d-a028-c0ec1721745f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245782} {:answer/id #uuid "d577a31d-6124-5fbe-949c-907e0f506fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246217} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d6cf3f-5569-5d37-8bae-a5a9fd24ed99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245681} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb99454a-a003-5139-9346-c2529872576e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fb853955-e8ef-5657-bd4c-789f3e409e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-112291" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -4 & -22\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=7 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ 8{x}_{3}=-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom third equation we get ${x}_{3}=-2$ and substituting this value into the second equation yields ${x}_{2}=-2(-2)+1=5.$ Finally, substituting ${x}_{2}=5$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+5=7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+5+(-2)=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "500d3e2f-f405-56db-9c0b-61c6afc8b340"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b3425c-3f4f-50ac-9c9d-0a2e7c77dbbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342384} {:answer/id #uuid "429a3619-daf6-5115-8c01-735449d7217d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342791} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca6b908-2e20-5cef-bb8b-aa67933e5937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343166} {:answer/id #uuid "7becf401-33da-536b-bc10-f41539c33323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343306} {:answer/id #uuid "868ebaf6-e3a5-54f9-a3a1-db42d06e7646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343397} :answer-field/id #uuid "737ac1c3-2191-5acf-acad-aaac5e91f2b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfe9fa20-a503-5468-a775-31a4b970b2ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-75973" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=4\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ \\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "620d8ecf-1f15-5e8d-9b5a-7ce1565395d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41cb02b9-13f3-5f1d-bb94-34c18d18a3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244889} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdc01cf-2d88-5f1d-918a-b6b8c908df28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245269} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddf9142-c44c-55fc-9780-62e19815fce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245422} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a4d81c-2941-5f57-a05a-cf24897f52ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246455} {:answer/id #uuid "b24cff0c-453c-54bf-b5a0-92ed0ff4ac54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245269} :answer-field/id #uuid "306483a6-036f-5c40-8757-606ab6fe2509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df51ea15-f830-55ce-a2e9-e42267e5f4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-112203" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,8\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=3\\cdot {b}_{1}+8\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{1}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "661bfcf4-d5c1-5b96-9534-b39e2fc11947" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06febdbc-65f9-5901-99f7-6a7827495ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426843} {:answer/id #uuid "5fac1657-f0bf-58cd-839a-2936f55e9617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426846} {:answer/id #uuid "922920ae-5499-5f5e-8c1f-2a7d78b98e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426848} {:answer/id #uuid "a26be051-5458-55a3-b657-078318cd500e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426849} {:answer/id #uuid "de386f32-9648-5557-95da-e81b5a893cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426852}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426849} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dbd70cc-1860-587f-a06b-f6be24d7a7e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad22bfb4-da83-594b-af3c-ca1a05478310" :question/math-academy-id "q-112247" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 15\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-3)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-4)+(-3)=-7.$"} :step/id #uuid "f56d58dc-6376-5d83-8bb7-3af73050ef21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426859}}] :activity/title "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426842}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455517} :activity/id #uuid "dd948f84-01c7-51cf-b5c9-f9c00a2b84b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638 :topic/id #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" :topic/math-academy-id 1864} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066bb7c4-7521-5727-993a-55db17b87282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454919} {:answer/id #uuid "49378edd-134f-5712-a720-7f1c131c32c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455026} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f74b12-935c-5e33-a5e5-220baef222b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455198} {:answer/id #uuid "b4852b6d-9a78-5d08-bc44-0fc169587a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455217} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fc417c-d758-5208-baf3-43da35bca066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455333}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455333} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa3925e-da8c-56e5-b9b6-c047f1edb4c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3d89aeef-7acc-5575-9764-44e144045f82" :question/math-academy-id "q-75998" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-7 \\\\ -3{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second   equation yields\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}-2(2)=-7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n Finally,  by substituting ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(3)+2=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSo, we have ${x}_{1}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "3120d902-59cf-52cd-afde-5f7a4f7e1d5f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028867b0-8cf6-524a-93e6-8e63fa02e23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317580} {:answer/id #uuid "2f329de5-7c1a-5fad-b2d2-f7cc8384731d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317709} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5bbc70-a2a7-5d4f-9c69-50122cef639d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317888} {:answer/id #uuid "844d0795-32e6-5eb9-9815-e5906f5fb3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318014} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c361cd-49f5-527f-91c1-eb17cbe67bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317580} :answer-field/id #uuid "add90972-43b4-50ff-b652-f7e992bc2ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f17a8e42-194f-52ed-9136-123dd348b4bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-34617" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-2)+2=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "3d7897f3-95d9-53e4-9b05-08455a50a2b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455528}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2115aac5-da66-5510-8b11-176ba754f81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409789} {:answer/id #uuid "45dbc0e6-16a9-5c62-a183-2dd30e767a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409790} {:answer/id #uuid "60013665-0a5a-5449-9017-6cb6ae7ed2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409792} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad6f309-f775-5604-ba46-fa9609a5688b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409798} {:answer/id #uuid "ba977b58-8add-5c57-ae69-640a36ab4ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409792} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe323417-1cf2-5a08-b6a1-9d36f7d769a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9b245f0-31aa-56fe-aa26-5663dd9e140f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112211" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,-8\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=(-8)\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-7)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-7.$"} :step/id #uuid "af36c254-27dd-5c17-9617-e7f9d654737f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f401e4-eb59-5198-abf4-1458830b331c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317692} {:answer/id #uuid "55e9e214-e0b3-5771-95e7-5cb487ad7118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317852} {:answer/id #uuid "914f6ffc-e4c9-5161-9fde-b475bcf8fceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318060} {:answer/id #uuid "e0273188-6c7e-5354-921c-cdc4386a3d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318313} {:answer/id #uuid "32fd85ec-3fca-5fdb-8d41-028482470a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454982}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317852} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0750bff-0d7b-54f9-bc9f-9e5f7a657747" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "706ae806-e20f-56d6-9072-359885047529" :question/math-academy-id "q-34738" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 22 & 22 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 22 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 22{x}_{2}=22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+4(1)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2+1=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "bc51db51-8852-5a95-a4d4-b77331fb8d8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2679ade7-de7e-5ff4-88c8-94968abbecd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454962} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4e66c0-8e5c-5dff-8789-8ac0302fb04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454974} {:answer/id #uuid "58225722-5487-53b4-b242-37a168921893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455056} {:answer/id #uuid "a19f8251-ccaa-52d8-8b83-bba63f64cb63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455191} {:answer/id #uuid "f7cbfdfe-250f-5f75-93f6-f1ca311bd2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454962} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c6024f5-9069-597b-a291-cd59361ac136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e3764343-10e9-5a7d-b034-e095faf7f51c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76054" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & 15 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -27\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=15 \\\\ -9{x}_{3}=-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting this into the second   equation yields\n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}+3(3)=15\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n Finally,  by substituting ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=3$ into the first equation we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3+3=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+3+3=9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e175e73c-1506-54af-ab5d-d11256d84f83" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455527}}] :activity/title "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454902}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483} :activity/id #uuid "3a675a3e-a62b-5b11-9c31-522dea85c72d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869 :topic/id #uuid "6cd92831-a889-52a8-bcc8-0868b82263c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1951} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\ \n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same." :tutorial/id #uuid "3d2fa632-58e8-5d68-a8e2-fb8822d211c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4256 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "063b4918-369c-5e01-892c-07d82af9b650" :step/math-academy-id 25881 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124569 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124378 :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421424}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "c64d2d4c-15fd-55c0-90c6-c2215440ba8d" :step/math-academy-id 25882 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124574 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124382 :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406698}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "65be1eef-6324-59ea-bbe6-d93f3c5b9154" :step/math-academy-id 26444}] :activity/title "Calculating Potential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124740}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421429} :activity/id #uuid "1a5e1aad-99c7-56df-9701-94352de75a5a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869 :topic/id #uuid "6cd92831-a889-52a8-bcc8-0868b82263c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1951} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61fd2894-92e2-5f21-a8ef-3e4ce0f75c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263843} {:answer/id #uuid "79e27da1-668f-5a0d-929e-edada25fd557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264058} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4936ba-0c0b-5085-a53f-9463a50276eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy+x{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264073} {:answer/id #uuid "ab37d3c3-0675-5158-a1cc-82765ce5104b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f7f29-86a2-537c-90f4-0eabb7389f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a355c26c-a760-5643-9305-ddb34ec96b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "062ec003-5729-5fe0-9819-864cef26e212" :question/math-academy-id "q-132100" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2})\\,j-2yz\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=-y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2yz & =-2yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15b0b7b5-261c-5133-9040-81b3cde4ef13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6c4808-c2a7-5c97-8912-c4f3de3ef57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} {:answer/id #uuid "7c064342-1507-5fe3-91dc-0ff15726350b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421419} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe7055-048b-506e-b0e8-41cb887f2884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421420} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8717c4-00ef-5595-aeca-97e8db51eb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421421} {:answer/id #uuid "c86c75a8-56c7-544d-9bc9-6d9a38de128c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f524e3-bd79-5da0-98c3-54cfe15b71e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49bf02f5-63a7-5afa-b61f-cb52dc9f8048" :question/math-academy-id "q-81738" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4-2xy)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2xy+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2xy+{g}^{′}(y) & =4-2xy \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =4 \\\\ g(y) & =4y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=4y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "49bc2690-9ce7-5797-ab91-ca25964951a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005532ea-a569-5e6b-9518-a0150b3fb329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz{e}^{z}+x+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263006} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7694c1-e825-574d-a180-e04f18376fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+x+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264082} {:answer/id #uuid "a49fbc3b-b203-5f1b-8f40-b0bcdbe9fbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{z}+y{x}^{2}+x-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264402} {:answer/id #uuid "c04be1f6-81c5-54c2-ba54-5b752c684e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+x+y-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264673} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d7254b-5172-5dfd-9ea1-9404e617cc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c4e81f-33ab-54ad-9dac-693d04696388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b7d90b3-0384-5231-a89f-b7683283ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-132103" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y{e}^{z})\\,i+(x{e}^{z}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{z}-2z)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{z}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{z}-2z & =xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "befa6245-a4cf-52d2-a6d8-3bd64bb32ca7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1602f189-0d28-56ae-9dd9-e128132e6ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}+yz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256980} {:answer/id #uuid "56a824e1-89ad-5b9f-9d90-0a15c3c6afec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz-y{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257529} {:answer/id #uuid "83844a24-d36a-5bde-90cc-25704c4bd024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} {:answer/id #uuid "9676b23d-58f9-5906-86c8-bc56f352ddbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258109} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfef173-d55d-5471-9bd3-2a9896819991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}z-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf730883-1f0b-511a-85de-b890c410b87c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af3748dd-98e9-5ccb-8f93-b1501896f383" :question/math-academy-id "q-124733" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+(3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z})\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2xy{z}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) & =3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c075c9d7-1b99-540c-b91d-b2920031d6d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32cd3a46-2070-5c04-b850-8162eeb789a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348898} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d8f137-d9d9-5d7a-bf57-3e496c94821f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350047} {:answer/id #uuid "cd48e39f-f120-599e-9a6b-494a2b1af79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350251} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d34f2d-177c-57dd-a130-fa54336633eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} {:answer/id #uuid "ed148d5a-2003-5ebe-a806-ce910794f76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=4xy+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c564c77-be41-5cc9-9524-b2c24202e626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3063cdc9-a985-5db1-8c3b-1970a3def78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81734" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(2{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y) & =2{x}^{2}y+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db028f1e-0fa4-5096-9db0-6954db49115e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421426}}] :activity/title "Calculating Potential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421417}]}
